# Reference reverse-proxy config for topcat.app.
#
# Why this file exists: the site has been served by uvicorn bound straight to
# the internet, over plain http. That means every search term and every show
# anyone looks at is readable by any network operator in the path, and — worse
# — an on-path attacker can rewrite the html. The Subresource Integrity hashes
# in app/templates/base.html are excellent, and they buy nothing at all over
# http, because a MITM simply deletes the integrity attributes before the
# browser ever sees them. Encrypting the transport is what makes the rest of
# the hardening real.
#
# Caddy is the recommendation because it gets and renews a Let's Encrypt
# certificate on its own, with no cron and no certbot to babysit.
#
#   sudo apt install caddy
#   sudo install -m 644 deploy/Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#   sudo systemctl reload caddy
#
# Requires ports 80 and 443 forwarded to the Pi, and topcat.app resolving to
# it. Caddy redirects http to https by itself; there is nothing to configure.
#
# If the Pi is behind CGNAT or a dynamic IP, use a Cloudflare Tunnel instead —
# same outcome, and no inbound port forwarding.

topcat.app {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
}

# Two changes belong on the uvicorn side once this is in front of it:
#
#   1. Bind to loopback, not 0.0.0.0. With Caddy terminating tls there is no
#      reason for uvicorn to accept a connection from anywhere else, and
#      leaving it on 0.0.0.0 means the plain-http port stays reachable and
#      quietly bypasses everything above.
#
#   2. Add --proxy-headers --forwarded-allow-ips=127.0.0.1 so the app logs the
#      real client instead of the proxy. Both flags together, always:
#      --proxy-headers on its own trusts X-Forwarded-For from anyone, which
#      hands every client the ability to forge its own source address.
#
#      uvicorn app.main:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000 \
#          --proxy-headers --forwarded-allow-ips=127.0.0.1
#
# And once https actually serves, set NH_WEBSITE_HSTS=1 in the service
# environment to turn on Strict-Transport-Security (see SEND_HSTS in
# app/main.py). Not before: HSTS is a year-long promise the browser caches, so
# sending it from a host that cannot serve https locks users out of the site.
